-- A One-Click opt-out is an opt-out, not a reply.
--
-- List-Unsubscribe One-Click arrives as a mail whose subject is "unsubscribe"
-- (OMI's own header asks for exactly that) and whose body is empty or a line of
-- provider boilerplate: "This message was automatically generated by Gmail."
-- Nobody typed it. The recipient pressed a button.
--
-- The first catch-up scan counted those as replies as well as opt-outs, which
-- would have stamped replied_at on every open send behind them — 8 sends on
-- some of October's — and inflated the reply rate with machine traffic. That is
-- the same class of wrong number as the gap this feature was built to close, so
-- trading one for the other would be no improvement.
--
-- Recorded per row rather than inferred at display time, because the body is
-- not kept and the judgement has to stay auditable after the fact.
ALTER TABLE outreach_reply_catchup
  ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS machine_opt_out BOOLEAN NOT NULL DEFAULT FALSE;

-- Rows already queued were judged by the old code, so they carry
-- mark_replied = true even where a mail client generated the message. Applying
-- them would do the exact damage this column exists to prevent.
--
-- Only PENDING rows go. Applied and dismissed rows are history and stay, and
-- their message_key keeps them from being proposed again. A re-scan recreates
-- the pending ones with the right flags; it is one button press and idempotent.
--
-- Deleting rather than back-filling in SQL is deliberate. The judgement lives in
-- isMachineOptOut(), which reads a phrase list shared with the live poll; a
-- second copy of that logic expressed as a regex here is exactly the kind of
-- drift that makes two parts of the system disagree about what an opt-out is.
DELETE FROM outreach_reply_catchup WHERE status = 'pending';
